WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.771
Gotham's grand finale will be no joke.

00:00:02.771 --> 00:00:07.509
A teaser trailer and character poster for
the series' final episode depict criminal

00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:11.660
mastermind Jeremiah Valeska in full-on Joker
Mode.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.510
It's highly unlikely he'll be referred to
as The Joker, but the promo materials leave

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:19.790
little doubt as to the fact that Jeremiah
will embrace his own destiny as the Clown

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:24.260
Prince of Crime during the 10-year time jump
which will see Bruce Wayne, played by David

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.550
Mazouz, take on the mantle of Batman.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:31.650
At least a portion of Gotham's final episode
will take place after that decade-long jump.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:37.010
As the spot, tellingly titled "J," opens,
there's Wayne Enterprises tower, looming over

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.810
a dark Gotham City as the tinkling strains
of Julie Andrews' "I Feel Pretty" glide over

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:42.810
the soundtrack.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.440
This gives way to a shot of Arkham Asylum.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.330
Inside its gates a seated figure with stringy,
patchy hair, remains still as the inmates

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:51.860
mill around in slow motion.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:57.490
Then, we see his face: it's Jeremiah Valeska,
played by Cameron Monaghan, looking worse

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:00.020
for wear after years presumably spent inside.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.160
"I feel something new.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.480
Something beautiful."

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.400
There's very familiar-sounding cackle over
the title card, followed by a brief shot of

00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:14.590
Jeremiah looking at his own hand, impaled
by a Batarang, with a mixture of alarm and

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:15.590
amusement.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.970
He bursts into hysterical laughter as the
spot ends.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:23.439
Jeremiah is clearly meant to be a version
of the Joker, if not necessarily the Joker.

00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:28.460
Fans have been engaged in back-and-forth debate
about whether Jeremiah, and before him, his

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.469
psychopathic twin brother Jerome would ever
become Gotham's iteration of the character,

00:01:32.469 --> 00:01:36.549
a debate which was seemingly laid to rest
when Monaghan tweeted a series of photos and

00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:39.360
videos from the makeup chair in May 2018.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.909
According to the actor, not only is he not
playing the Joker, but the show is prohibited

00:01:43.909 --> 00:01:46.899
by Warner Brothers from using the character
in any way.

00:01:46.899 --> 00:01:51.759
Executive producer John Stephens even flatly
stated as much himself in a conversation with

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.360
IGN later that year, saying,

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:06.520
The real Joker, Stephens says, is out there,
taking his cues from proto-Jokers including

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.470
Jeremiah, Jerome, and others.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:12.820
Stephens then seemed to at least partially
walk back his comments in a GameSpot interview

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.140
in March 2019.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:28.550
Stephens added that there are still other
traits of the Joker's personality, such as

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:32.360
being "actually a nightmare," that the Gotham
writers had yet to explore.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.700
This version of the maybe-Joker may fit that
bill.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.920
He has a look nearly as terrifying as Heath
Ledger's iconic interpretation in The Dark

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.420
Knight, and that chilling laugh is indeed
the stuff of nightmares.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:47.710
One can't help but feel that Fox is basically
doing an end run, sticking to the letter of

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:51.780
Warner Brothers' mandate while finding a way
to pair Batman, who is finally making his

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:56.290
appearance on Gotham after five seasons and
99 episodes, with his nemesis.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:01.160
With the final episode, fans will finally
get to see Mazouz in action as the Caped Crusader,

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:05.050
or at least his face digitally pasted onto
the body of a much more physically imposing

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:08.490
stand-in, opposed by a very Joker-like villain.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:12.650
Stephens and company can insist that Jeremiah
is just a prototype Joker who happened to

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:17.170
have fallen into a vat of toxic chemicals
in the exact same manner as Jack Nicholson's

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:19.080
Joker in Tim Burton's Batman.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.460
And that he just happens to look, sound, and
act exactly like the criminal who he will

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:28.620
inspire with his maniacal ways at some undetermined
point in a future Gotham will never show.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:33.190
Evidently, Warner Brothers' brass are buying
it, but that doesn't mean viewers have to.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.680
Gotham's penultimate episode, "They Did What?"

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.440
airs on April 18; the finale, "The Beginning…"
follows April 25.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:41.790
Mark your calendars.

